{"title":"Derangement","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>A <b>derangement</b> is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Permutation\" title=\"Permutation\">permutation</a> with no <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Fixed_point\" title=\"Fixed point\">fixed points</a>.  That is, a derangement of a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Set\" title=\"Set\">set</a> leaves no <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Element\" title=\"Element\">element</a> in its original place.  For example, the derangements of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/f/fef839af6c8716130345f2d29030096bb4c2ac22.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1,2,3\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"18\" /> are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/0/3c06c65cfcec90feacb3a066a509bc98bc7ca414.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{2, 3, 1\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/d/47dd7d02e661aa7241959a3fd2234589dfae086e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{3, 1, 2\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"18\" />, but <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dcecd6680abbf8b4dbaedae3b59e7f219eea9fb1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{3,2, 1\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"63\" height=\"20\" /> is not a derangement of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/f/fef839af6c8716130345f2d29030096bb4c2ac22.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1,2,3\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"18\" /> because 2 is a fixed point.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Notation_and_formula\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Notation and formula</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Proof_of_Formula\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof of Formula</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Limit_as_n_approaches\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Limit as n approaches</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Related_Problems\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Related Problems</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Notation_and_formula\">Notation and formula</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Derangement&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Notation and formula\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The number of derangements of an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-element set is called the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />th derangement number or <i>rencontres number</i>, or the <i>subfactorial</i> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and is sometimes denoted <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/c/cbc1e4110bc75705c10ccfa39ecd701c91abbc9c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$!n$\" width=\"15\" height=\"13\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/a/2eacbcb991af87d495f8689ac0e952b51317dbaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"23\" height=\"15\" />.  (Note that using this notation may require some care, as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/3/95395efa3323fe277a92f30c494e9277e54fe471.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a!b$\" width=\"22\" height=\"13\" /> can potentially mean both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/5/bf509de3518d9d00d6aa23e1ae428a94d5d93b5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a!)b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/9/449be5405fecb35e3d1a797c39be1ae75eb37342.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a(!b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" />.)  This number satisfies the recurrences\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/b/77bbc2c06218239b21273269fae4f49949611b9b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[!n = n \\cdot !(n - 1) + (-1)^n\\]\" width=\"183\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>and\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/7/a47f5599c71b6cd229e2892334e2e2a67e996920.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[!n = (n - 1)\\cdot (!(n - 1) + !(n - 2))\\]\" width=\"257\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>and is given by the formula\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/d/a6dc5b03b0f0635ae5384b8a78967ac7ae367890.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[!n = n! \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\frac{(-1)^k}{k!}.\\]\" width=\"139\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>For example, the number of derangements of a 3-element set is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/e/6eecd822487f1e5ba969b77abdfbe9f704e308db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3! \\cdot \\sum_{k = 0}^3 \\frac{(-1)^k}{k!} = 6\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{1} - \\frac{1}{1} + \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{6}\\right) = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"330\" height=\"50\" />, which we know to be correct.\n</p><p>The first few derangements, starting from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/3/02349576cee613512cdf301a365f06c0760acab5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=0$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/a/0bacfe80cb02a67cd72c21583400680ff31a92db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1, 0, 1, 2, 9, 44, 265, 1854, 14833, 133496, 1334961, 14684570, \\ldots.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"488\" height=\"16\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof_of_Formula\">Proof of Formula</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Derangement&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Proof of Formula\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>This can be proven using PIE.  First, you take <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63f344e7d8b14a7886fc7fb72da53cd0a3271dcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, which represents all arrangements of the whole sequence.  Then, you must subtract all arrangements in which each element appears in its original location, and now you have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/f/8bfa7f957b4f7daea1cb6011eb14cf5b4ab1e9e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!-\\binom{n}{1}(n-1)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"137\" height=\"53\" />.  Then, you must add back in permutations in which each set of two elements stay in their original positions, as we subtracted them twice.  Now, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/a/d6ab17f5697baca1f5edf50719c2bfb5c5fed335.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!-\\binom{n}{1}(n-1)!+\\binom{n}{2}(n-2)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"259\" height=\"53\" />.  PIE continues to give us this pattern.  We have to alternate between subtracting and adding.\n</p><p>Now, we can change the form of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/b/e8bfeb033ec7fc752d74c4e7d5e43dc2e27c4322.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{n}{k}(n-k)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"100\" height=\"53\" />.  This is written as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/7/6572952b53ee9eb729030465ec4d39176f2730f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\\times(n-k)!=\\frac{n!}{k!}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"208\" height=\"42\" />.  We can now write our relation as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/1/7a154d995149cf78e4a871a1572a3313636aad4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!-\\frac{n!}{1!}+\\frac{n!}{2!}-\\frac{n!}{3!}+...+(-1)^n(\\frac{n!}{n!})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"281\" height=\"38\" />.  We can factor out <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63f344e7d8b14a7886fc7fb72da53cd0a3271dcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> and get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/0/950719c085e81b76c4dea6bc653cd61aff99aeb4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n!(1-\\frac{1}{1!}+\\frac{1}{2!}-\\frac{1}{3!}+...+(-1)^n\\frac{1}{n!})=n!\\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\frac{(-1)^k}{k!}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"406\" height=\"48\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Limit_as_n_approaches\">Limit as n approaches <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/7/b671f1bb7e4ee86584347d5d22f1dc8abdb5bef2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\infty$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"9\" /></span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Derangement&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Limit as n approaches\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>From the recurrence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/d/75d29ace88e363316ed5e1c696d6c020f32355d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$!n=n\\cdot!(n-1)+(-1)^{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"185\" height=\"18\" />, we can find that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/f/5cf34e0f20d05708697bb772305bca16757c73f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac{!n}{n!} =\\frac{1}{e} \\approx 0.3679\\ldots.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"200\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>Also, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/2/4229707913d8a78f60d375cfc15c784bea58db34.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$!n = \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n!}{e}+\\frac{1}{2}\\right\\rfloor.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"122\" height=\"43\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Related_Problems\">Related Problems</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Derangement&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Related Problems\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=University_of_South_Carolina_High_School_Math_Contest/1993_Exam/Problem_11\" title=\"University of South Carolina High School Math Contest/1993 Exam/Problem 11\"> 1993 University of South Carolina Math Contest Problem 11</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_PMWC_Problems/Problem_I11\" title=\"2005 PMWC Problems/Problem I11\"> 2005 PMWC Problem I11</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 11\"> 2025 AMC 10B Problem 11</a></li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Derangement&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Correspondence\" title=\"Correspondence\">Correspondence</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Derangement&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018082400\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.023 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.038 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 181/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 274/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 6145/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.778      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.778      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:3924:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018082400 and revision id 279785. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}